1
00:00:00,260 --> 00:00:08,220
Imagine looking at, uh, your organization's IT infrastructure and just seeing this maze of, you know, fragmented, duplicated systems.

2
00:00:08,480 --> 00:00:10,980
Yeah, systems that are incredibly costly to change.

3
00:00:11,420 --> 00:00:11,920
Exactly.

4
00:00:12,560 --> 00:00:20,340
And transforming that kind of siloed bureaucracy, well, it usually comes with a massive multi-year roadmap that promises everything, but

5
00:00:20,880 --> 00:00:22,700
honestly proves absolutely nothing.

6
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:27,340
Right, which is exactly why we're exploring the GovStack reference architecture today.

7
00:00:27,840 --> 00:00:29,620
Yeah. Welcome to today's deep dive, everyone.

8
00:00:30,120 --> 00:00:39,680
Our mission is to unpack their strategy for education, specifically looking at how you can deliver high priority services to citizens in just months by focusing on a single

9
00:00:39,900 --> 00:00:41,580
foundational first proof.

10
00:00:42,280 --> 00:00:45,140
And that first proof strategy is just so crucial.

11
00:00:45,960 --> 00:00:51,120
Instead of boiling the ocean, GovStack's second phase says, "Hey, just pick one critical service."

12
00:00:51,600 --> 00:00:52,740
Prototype it quickly, right?

13
00:00:53,140 --> 00:00:56,580
Exactly. Prototype it, and have a local system integrator roll it out.

14
00:00:57,300 --> 00:01:01,420
For education, this first proof is simply registering a learner once.

15
00:01:02,200 --> 00:01:07,240
Because, I mean, families interact with that process the moment a child starts school, so it makes sense.

16
00:01:07,940 --> 00:01:11,500
Every subsequent education service relies on that initial record existing.

17
00:01:12,040 --> 00:01:15,000
Yes, but here is the really interesting part.

18
00:01:15,600 --> 00:01:18,760
The architecture requires two separate building blocks for this.

19
00:01:19,720 --> 00:01:22,840
Right, a registration block and a digital registry block.

20
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:23,480
But wait,

21
00:01:24,240 --> 00:01:27,040
splitting the registration process from the actual database?

22
00:01:27,460 --> 00:01:29,960
That sounds like you're just building two systems instead of one.

23
00:01:30,160 --> 00:01:31,600
Doesn't that double the work?

24
00:01:32,080 --> 00:01:35,560
Well, separating them is actually the key to avoiding future silos.

25
00:01:36,280 --> 00:01:38,960
The registration block actively handles the workflow.

26
00:01:40,100 --> 00:01:42,860
So the actual doing, you know, the processing of forms.

27
00:01:43,200 --> 00:01:45,880
Precisely. It handles the forms and approvals.

28
00:01:46,560 --> 00:01:52,320
Meanwhile, the digital registry block quietly acts as the authoritative database, the storing part.

29
00:01:53,280 --> 00:02:00,920
Ah, I get it. Because if you combine them, every time you update the, uh, website interface, you risk crashing your master database.

30
00:02:01,220 --> 00:02:09,400
Exactly. Separating it means that database can be securely reused by a dozen other departments later without anyone touching the registration interface.

31
00:02:10,220 --> 00:02:11,000
That makes total sense.

32
00:02:11,860 --> 00:02:17,660
So looking at how this single service functions, it actually relies on four building blocks working together, right?

33
00:02:18,140 --> 00:02:22,260
Yes, four building blocks united by a data exchange layer.

34
00:02:22,800 --> 00:02:27,600
And the education sector builds its own two blocks, which are registration and the learner register.

35
00:02:28,080 --> 00:02:32,800
But it completely reuses the country's existing building blocks for identity and payments.

36
00:02:33,280 --> 00:02:36,800
Which means you aren't reinventing the wheel or, you know, getting locked into one supplier.

37
00:02:37,160 --> 00:02:37,380
Right.

38
00:02:38,120 --> 00:02:42,080
I like to think of that data exchange layer as a sort of universal translator.

39
00:02:42,380 --> 00:02:51,180
A translator? Yeah. Like, the education department speaks one technical language, the tax department speaks another, but this layer lets them instantly trade data.

40
00:02:51,480 --> 00:02:58,660
Oh, absolutely. So a parent can sign in using the national identity block to register a child, and once an officer approves it,

41
00:02:59,040 --> 00:03:02,080
that record is instantly linked to the national payments block.

42
00:03:02,680 --> 00:03:06,520
And a scholarship payment can then seamlessly reach that specific learner's record.

43
00:03:06,740 --> 00:03:08,580
No data rekeyed or captured twice.

44
00:03:08,960 --> 00:03:10,460
Which is huge for efficiency.

45
00:03:11,060 --> 00:03:14,380
Definitely. But what does this actually look like in practice?

46
00:03:14,960 --> 00:03:18,680
The GovStack model uses a fictional country called Progresa to demonstrate this, right?

47
00:03:19,020 --> 00:03:21,480
Right. Progresa is a fictional example country.

48
00:03:22,080 --> 00:03:26,600
In Progresa, they fully reuse the existing identity block and the data exchange layer.

49
00:03:26,940 --> 00:03:31,660
But the learner registry is built from scratch specifically for the education sector.

50
00:03:32,020 --> 00:03:37,220
Exactly. And payments are set up and joined to the data exchange to reach the national payment provider.

51
00:03:37,920 --> 00:03:41,560
Other things like consent and messaging are just cited, not built from scratch.

52
00:03:42,160 --> 00:03:42,500
But wait,

53
00:03:43,180 --> 00:03:47,480
building the learner registry now, doesn't that violate the architecture's own rules?

54
00:03:48,140 --> 00:03:48,520
How do you mean?

55
00:03:49,080 --> 00:03:54,780
Well, digitalizing main state registries is supposed to be reserved for phase three much later in the roadmap.

56
00:03:55,200 --> 00:03:55,540
Oh, right.

57
00:03:56,180 --> 00:03:58,000
Well, it is a strategic exception.

58
00:03:58,780 --> 00:04:01,440
Because the priority service literally cannot work without it.

59
00:04:01,820 --> 00:04:08,840
Exactly. This specific register is deliberately brought forward to prove one priority service on the country's foundation.

60
00:04:09,380 --> 00:04:11,120
It doesn't replace the broader national plan.

61
00:04:11,740 --> 00:04:15,780
So the ultimate takeaway here is that you have to prove one service works first.

62
00:04:16,320 --> 00:04:22,280
You register a learner once, combining the sector's new service with existing identity, payment, and data exchange blocks.

63
00:04:23,220 --> 00:04:29,040
You avoid those fragmented, costly to change silos by proving the integration works on a manageable scale.

64
00:04:29,600 --> 00:04:30,160
Absolutely.

65
00:04:30,920 --> 00:04:34,120
So to wrap up, think about your own professional world.

